(D2 — Discrepancy 2 repro: LANGUAGE.md:4262 says the device-node statement
 "Sets the error flag on failure", which places the failure at run time. A
 bad `type` is rejected at COMPILE time instead, so `On error` never sees
 it and the program never runs. Both behaviours are defensible; the manual
 documents only one of them.
 The diagnostic is also where the undocumented `"p"` of D1 surfaces.
 Ran, from the repo root:
   VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm \
   /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox \
   docs/ledger/probes/process-control/D2.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   error: Invalid device type 'x' - expected "c" (character), "b" (block), or "p" (FIFO)
Create a directory called "vf_scratch".
Create a directory called "vf_scratch/process-control".
   (no binary produced; compile error)
)
Create a device node called "vf_scratch/process-control/scratch_weird" with type "x" major 1 minor 3.
On error print "this handler is never reached".
